        "heading": "1 INTRODUCTION",
        "text": "Let \u03c0 be a probability density on Rd of the form\n\u03c0(x) = \u03b3(x)\nZ , Z = \u222b Rd \u03b3(x)dx, (1)\nwhere \u03b3 : Rd \u2192 R+ can be evaluated pointwise but the normalizing constant Z is intractable. We are here interested in both estimating Z and obtaining approximate samples from \u03c0.\nA large variety of Monte Carlo techniques has been developed in the literature to address this problem. In particular Annealed Importance Sampling (AIS) (Neal, 2001) and its Sequential Monte Carlo (SMC) extensions (Del Moral et al., 2006) are often regarded as the \u201cgold standard\u201d approach to compute normalizing constants. These methods rely on the introduction of a sequence of annealed versions of \u03c0 which are explored using a combination of Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC), importance sampling and resampling steps.\nVariational techniques are a popular alternative to MCMC and SMC where one considers a flexible family of easy-to-sample distributions q\u03b8 whose parameters are optimized by minimizing a suitable metric w.r.t. \u03c0, typically the reverse Kullback\u2013Leibler discrepancy KL(q\u03b8||\u03c0). Typical choices for q\u03b8 include mean-field approximation (Wainwright & Jordan, 2008) or more sophisticated techniques such as normalizing flows (Papamakarios et al., 2019). To be able to model complex variational distributions, it has often proven useful to model q\u03b8(x) as the marginal of an auxiliary extended distribution; i.e. q\u03b8(x) = \u222b q\u03b8(x, u)du. As this marginal is typically intractable, \u03b8 is then learned by minimizing a discrepancy measure between q\u03b8(x, u) and an extended target distribution p\u03b8(x, u) = \u03c0(x)p\u03b8(u|x) where p\u03b8(u|x) is an auxiliary conditional distribution (Agakov & Barber, 2004). Over recent years, variational techniques have also been used fruitfully to optimize the algorithmic parameters of Monte Carlo techniques. For example, AIS can be thought of a procedure where q\u03b8(x, u) is the joint distribution of a Markov chain defined by a sequence of MCMC kernels whose final state is x while p\u03b8(x, u) is the corresponding AIS extended target distribution (Neal, 2001). The parameters \u03b8 of these kernels can then be optimized by minimizing KL(q\u03b8||p\u03b8) using stochastic\ngradient (Wu et al., 2020; Geffner & Domke, 2021; Thin et al., 2021; Zhang et al., 2021; Doucet et al., 2022; Geffner & Domke, 2022).\nIn this work, we follow an approach inspired by Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models (DDPM), a powerful class of generative models (Sohl-Dickstein et al., 2015; Ho et al., 2020; Song et al., 2021b). In this context, one adds noise progressively to data using a diffusion to transform the complex data distribution into a Gaussian distribution. The time-reversal of this diffusion can then be used to transform a Gaussian sample into a sample from the target. While superficially similar to Langevin dynamics, this process mixes fast even in high dimensions as it inherits the mixing properties of the forward diffusion (De Bortoli et al., 2021). However, as the time-reversal involves the logarithmic derivatives of the intractable marginal densities of the forward diffusion, these so-called scores are practically approximated using score matching techniques. If the score estimation error is small, the approximate time-reversal still enjoys remarkable theoretical properties (De Bortoli, 2022; Chen et al., 2022; Lee et al., 2022).\nThese results motivate us to introduce Denoising Diffusion Samplers (DDS). Like DDPM, we consider a forward diffusion which progressively transforms the target \u03c0 into a Gaussian distribution. This defines an extended target distribution p(x, u) = \u03c0(x)p(u|x). DDS are obtained by approximating the time-reversal of this diffusion using a process of distribution q\u03b8(x, u). What distinguishes DDS from DDPM is that we cannot simulate sample paths from the diffusion we want to time-reverse, as its initial state x is distributed according to \u03c0. Hence score matching ideas cannot be used to approximate the score terms and we focused here on minimizing KL(q\u03b8||p) as in variational inference. However, we show how one can still exploit the many similarities between DDS and DDPM to leverage some of the ideas developed in generative modeling for Monte Carlo sampling. This includes using the probability flow ordinary differential equation (ODE) (Song et al., 2021b) to derive novel normalizing flows and the use underdamped diffusions as a forward noising diffusion (Dockhorn et al., 2022). Theoretical results for DDPM also provide theoretical guarantees for DDS."
